### Describe the bug

When a keyed `{#each}` reconcile removes several rows at once, `destroy_effects` (`internal/client/dom/blocks/each.js`) destroys them in a plain loop:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < to_destroy.length; i++) {
  var e = to_destroy[i];
  ...
  destroy_effect(to_destroy[i], remove_dom);
}
```

`$effect` teardowns run inside `destroy_effect` (`execute_effect_teardown`), so a **throwing teardown in one row aborts the loop and the rest of the batch is never destroyed**. The stranded rows:

- are gone from the each block's item bookkeeping, so **later reconciles never revisit them** (verified: the teardown throws exactly once; the stranded subscriptions then persist unchanged across dozens of subsequent reconciles);
- stay subscribed to their dependencies and retain their (now detached) DOM.

They are still linked in the effect tree, so destroying the whole `{#each}` (e.g. final `unmount()`) does sweep them. But for the canonical case — a virtualized list/feed whose view lives as long as the app does — the leak's lifetime is effectively the session: every recurring teardown throw strands another row subtree, unbounded.

The same abort-unsafe shape exists one level down: `destroy_effect_children` clears `signal.first`/`signal.last` **before** walking the children, so a mid-walk throw orphans the unwalked remainder of any destroy pass (the parent has already forgotten them). The repro below demonstrates the batch loop specifically.

### Reproduction

https://github.com/randalmurphal/svelte-throw-leak-repros — folder `issue2-teardown-throw-strand/` (`npm install && npm test`; the control test passes, the bug test fails on 5.56.3 and 5.55.4 showing the stranded subscriber).

Three small files — a 6-row window slid by 2 rows per step over a keyed `{#each}`, so each reconcile destroys a batch of 2 leaving rows:

