### Describe the bug

When a reaction (template effect or derived) throws **after reading a never-before-evaluated derived** in the same update, that derived is leaked permanently:

1. In `get()` (`internal/client/runtime.js`), a fresh derived read inside an updating effect takes the `should_connect` path: it gets the `CONNECTED` flag **before** `update_derived`, so its own `update_reaction` pushes it into all of its dependencies' `reactions` arrays (the `(deps[i].reactions ??= []).push(reaction)` block).
2. The *reader*, however, only registers itself into the derived's `reactions` at the end of the reader's own `update_reaction` (same post-block). If the reader's `fn` throws first, that never happens.
3. Result: the derived is `CONNECTED`, present in its deps' `reactions` arrays, with `reactions === null` of its own. The disconnect cascade in `remove_reaction` can never fire for it — there is no reader whose removal would empty its reactions. `destroy_effect` doesn't help either: destroy walks **effects'** deps, and no effect ever depended on this derived.

The leaked derived (and everything its `ctx`/closures retain — component context, DOM) stays reachable through the dependency signal **forever**, surviving destruction of the owning component and even `unmount()` of the whole app.

Real-world impact: in an app where one row component had a recurring render-time throw inside a virtualized list (rows recreated on every scroll-in), a single long session accumulated **1,499 instances of one compiler-generated prop derived** stuck in one store signal's `reactions` array, transitively retaining ~112 MB (detached DOM + component contexts). Chrome's "Detached elements" view undercounts this because the retained DOM hangs off JS contexts rather than detached-tree roots. In a real app the triggering throw is usually caught by an error boundary or surfaces as an uncaught window error while the rest of the UI keeps working — so the leak compounds silently for as long as the session lives.

### Reproduction

https://github.com/randalmurphal/svelte-throw-leak-repros — folder `issue1-render-throw-derived-leak/` (`npm install && npm test`; the control test passes, the bug test fails on 5.56.3 and 5.55.4 with the leaked subscriber retained).

The full repro is three small files:

```svelte
